WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.459 --> 00:00:04.690
Speaker 1: We got some very interesting news
yesterday about the American relationship

00:00:04.690 --> 00:00:06.520
with Saudi Arabia.

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:09.800
If you've been listening to this show for
a while, you've been watching the show for

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:10.800
a while.

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:15.410
You know that I have been saying for, what,
a decade and a half the United States really

00:00:15.410 --> 00:00:18.230
needs to reevaluate its relationship with
Saudi Arabia.

00:00:18.230 --> 00:00:21.859
Now we understand why the relationship exists.

00:00:21.859 --> 00:00:28.140
There is a sort of general shared interest
in calm in the Middle East between the United

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:29.980
States and Saudi Arabia.

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:34.750
And this relates to oil prices and oil production
and for other reasons.

00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:39.920
And that has been a major factor in the relationship
between the U.S. and Saudi Arabia.

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:42.050
Cut to the New York Times yesterday.

00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:48.290
Joe Biden vows consequences for Saudi Arabia
after oil production cut.

00:00:48.290 --> 00:00:49.290
The article reads.

00:00:49.290 --> 00:00:53.180
Angered by the kingdom's decision to team
up with Russia.

00:00:53.180 --> 00:00:59.329
President Biden signaled openness to retaliatory
measures, including a halt to arms sales and

00:00:59.329 --> 00:01:01.429
allowing price fixing lawsuits.

00:01:01.429 --> 00:01:08.030
This is a very good article in The Times by
Peter Baker, and it reads that Biden vowed

00:01:08.030 --> 00:01:13.979
yesterday to impose consequences on Saudi
Arabia, signaling a rupture in the relationship

00:01:13.979 --> 00:01:19.630
between two longtime allies and a reversal
of Biden's own effort to cultivate the energy

00:01:19.630 --> 00:01:21.030
rich kingdom.

00:01:21.030 --> 00:01:27.509
This all relates to last week's decision by
Saudi led OPEC opec+.

00:01:27.509 --> 00:01:35.009
A Biden staff announced he would, quote, reevaluate
the entire relationship with Saudi Arabia

00:01:35.009 --> 00:01:41.369
and expressed openness to retaliatory measures
offered by congressional Dems, such as curbing

00:01:41.369 --> 00:01:46.609
arms sales or permitting legal action against
the cartel.

00:01:46.609 --> 00:01:51.700
This is as much about the direct relationship
with Saudi Arabia as it is about the Russian

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:53.619
war in Ukraine.

00:01:53.619 --> 00:01:57.200
And we have to see whether these are empty,
empty words or not.

00:01:57.200 --> 00:01:58.200
Are these empty threats?

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:00.560
Are these just vague promises that will never
become anything?

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:03.450
Or is this something real again?

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:09.190
I did not have particularly high hopes for
Joe Biden when I decided I would support him

00:02:09.190 --> 00:02:10.770
over Donald Trump.

00:02:10.770 --> 00:02:15.310
He was the obviously correct decision over
Donald Trump for any sane person, even remotely

00:02:15.310 --> 00:02:16.900
tethered to reality.

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:20.170
But my expectations were quite muted about
Joe Biden.

00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:25.470
And despite a period of about three or four
months, five or six months, maybe even during

00:02:25.470 --> 00:02:29.670
which very little that I could point to and
say this is a good thing has happened.

00:02:29.670 --> 00:02:33.830
Joe Biden's presidency has really sort of
taken a turn for the better.

00:02:33.830 --> 00:02:39.190
And it would be another great thing from my
perspective if Joe Biden were to reevaluate

00:02:39.190 --> 00:02:41.340
this relationship.

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:43.300
Consider the reality of Saudi Arabia.

00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:46.440
I mean, first of all, funded 911.

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:53.720
Most hijackers were Saudi killed and dismembered
Saudi journalist Jamal Khashoggi.

00:02:53.720 --> 00:02:56.890
There has been essentially no accountability.

00:02:56.890 --> 00:03:01.730
War in Yemen with massive human rights abuses,
by the way, I mean, with some American support.

00:03:01.730 --> 00:03:07.500
The United States hands are not exactly clean
of blood there either.

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:14.890
Important to measure, to mention state sponsored
theocratic misogyny and oppression, although

00:03:14.890 --> 00:03:20.150
it has improved slightly over the last ten
years economically supporting Russia in their

00:03:20.150 --> 00:03:22.480
war in Ukraine.

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:29.490
This has been an alliance for oil purposes
and for general interest in stability in the

00:03:29.490 --> 00:03:35.690
Middle East purposes, and in particular with
the United States.

00:03:35.690 --> 00:03:41.370
I mean, listen, is it as fast a move away
from fossil fuels as I would like?

00:03:41.370 --> 00:03:43.320
No, not nearly.

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:48.850
But in particular, with the growing priority
of the United States under Joe Biden, much

00:03:48.850 --> 00:03:54.990
more so than under Donald Trump of furthering
alternative and renewable energy usage and

00:03:54.990 --> 00:03:57.630
getting away from fossil fuels.

00:03:57.630 --> 00:04:05.370
There is less of a need, in a sense, to be
cozy with what is a very backwards, dictatorial

00:04:05.370 --> 00:04:06.660
state.

00:04:06.660 --> 00:04:12.720
So if we were able to detach ourselves from
the I guess we'll call it the teat of Saudi

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:15.750
oil, it's a good thing long term.

00:04:15.750 --> 00:04:21.229
Now, the truth is that the global situation
is much more complicated than I'm making it

00:04:21.229 --> 00:04:23.210
out to be in this segment.

00:04:23.210 --> 00:04:25.030
There are many other considerations.

00:04:25.030 --> 00:04:28.919
There are all sorts of triangles in which
you have the United States and Saudi Arabia

00:04:28.919 --> 00:04:33.870
and some other country, which are important
considerations as well from a foreign policy

00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:34.870
standpoint.

00:04:34.870 --> 00:04:41.210
But it would be one of the greatest things
Joe Biden could do to cool that relationship

00:04:41.210 --> 00:04:42.210
off.

00:04:42.210 --> 00:04:44.490
It's been something I've been calling for
for a very long time now.

00:04:44.490 --> 00:04:49.990
In the short term, the problem is that this
action by Saudi Arabia will cause energy prices

00:04:49.990 --> 00:04:51.430
to go up.

00:04:51.430 --> 00:04:57.240
And if Joe Biden did indeed take some substantive,
substantive step to cool that relationship

00:04:57.240 --> 00:05:00.889
and to reevaluate, it will probably cause
energy prices to go up even more.

00:05:00.889 --> 00:05:05.860
And we're getting into winter where home energy
prices are an important thing.

00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:11.960
The increase in energy prices will almost
certainly be used against Joe Biden in 2024.

00:05:11.960 --> 00:05:17.830
Republicans will be able to say, Listen, Biden
chose to do this with Saudi Arabia and this

00:05:17.830 --> 00:05:21.870
made everybody at home energy costs more and
gas you all the there.

00:05:21.870 --> 00:05:25.880
There's no doubt that Republicans will try
to do that.

00:05:25.880 --> 00:05:28.460
It's easy to say, well, it's worth it anyway.

00:05:28.460 --> 00:05:33.110
Well, for some people, it's an extraordinarily
difficult situation to have an increase in

00:05:33.110 --> 00:05:37.100
gas prices or home energy prices, home heating
prices, etc..

00:05:37.100 --> 00:05:41.050
That will be in effect and it will be they
will try to use it against Joe Biden.

00:05:41.050 --> 00:05:47.919
At some point, the decision has to be made
to back away from this absolutely horrifying

00:05:47.919 --> 00:05:50.569
relationship with Saudi Arabia.

00:05:50.569 --> 00:05:53.730
And I always say there is no better time than
the present.

00:05:53.730 --> 00:05:55.789
If you disagree with me, let me know.

